endobj
504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 66 0 R
/T (7.1 Effect of k on Purity)
/E (7.1 Effect of k on Purity)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 66 0 R
/T (7.2 Effect of k on Rand Index )
/E (7.2 Effect of k on Rand Index )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (The Purity score generally improved as the number of SOM neurons increased from k = 2 to k = 9. The highest Purity value was achieved at k = 9 with a score of 0.9574, indicating that the SOM was able to form highly homogeneous clusters with minimal category mixing.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
507 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (At lower values of k, especially k = 2 and k = 3, multiple semantic categories were grouped into the same clusters, which reduced clustering purity. As k increased, the SOM allocated more neurons to represent the semantic structure of the dataset more effectively.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
508 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (For k = 4, which matches the actual number of categories in the dataset, the model achieved strong performance with Purity = 0.9453 and Rand Index = 0.9638. The contingency matrix shows that most categories were clearly separated, particularly the Countries and Animals categories.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (When k became larger than the true number of categories, some categories were divided into multiple smaller clusters. This increased Purity slightly, but the Rand Index gradually decreased because the clustering became more fragmented.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (The Rand Index reached its highest value at k = 4 with a score of 0.9638. This result is reasonable because the dataset contains four true semantic categories, allowing the SOM to produce clusters that closely match the ground-truth labels.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (At lower values of k, several categories were grouped together in the same clusters, which reduced the agreement between the predicted clusters and the true labels. As k increased beyond 4, the SOM began dividing some categories into smaller sub-clusters. Although this slightly improved Purity, it caused the Rand Index to decrease because words from the same category were distributed across multiple clusters.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
512 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 66 0 R
/E (These results demonstrate the trade-off between Purity and Rand Index. Larger k values improve cluster homogeneity, while k values closer to the true number of classes provide better agreement with the actual category labels.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
